These are the callers that get restricted. If the current process is blacklisted, MasterHide hides protected targets from it, denies some access, and spoofs some hardware-query results: [globals.hpp (line 52)](/C:/Users/hallo/Downloads/MasterHide/MasterHide/globals.hpp:52), [mh_hooks.cpp (line 165)](/C:/Users/hallo/Downloads/MasterHide/MasterHide/mh_hooks.cpp:165), [mh_hooks.cpp (line 310)](/C:/Users/hallo/Downloads/MasterHide/MasterHide/mh_hooks.cpp:310), [mh_hooks.cpp (line 626)](/C:/Users/hallo/Downloads/MasterHide/MasterHide/mh_hooks.cpp:626)

By default in this tree, the built-in lists are very small:
protected processes: cheatengine, ProcessHacker: [globals.hpp (line 36)](/C:/Users/hallo/Downloads/MasterHide/MasterHide/globals.hpp:36)
protected drivers: dbk64, processhacker2: [globals.hpp (line 26)](/C:/Users/hallo/Downloads/MasterHide/MasterHide/globals.hpp:26)
